\details{
Per parameter (the sampled components and, with \code{derived = TRUE}, the heritability
computed draw by draw as in \code{summary()} and the correlation of every \code{cov()} with its two
variances, since a failure can show only in a nonlinear function of the components):
\itemize{
\item \code{rhat}: the rank-normalized split R-hat of Vehtari et al. (2021), the larger of the bulk
and the folded (tail) versions; \code{rhat_bulk} and \code{rhat_tail} separately.
\item \code{ess_bulk}, \code{ess_tail}: the multi-chain effective sample sizes of the same paper. The
chains share one autocorrelation estimate, so chains that disagree LOWER the ESS (a sum
of per-chain ESS cannot see that).
\code{ess_bulk_chain_min} is the smallest per-chain bulk-ESS. \code{disagreement} is the sum of
the per-chain bulk-ESS divided by the multi-chain one: near 1 when the chains agree,
large when the between-chain variance exceeds what their autocorrelation predicts.
\code{disagreement_tail} is the same ratio on the folded draws \verb{|x - median|}, which sees
chains that agree in location and differ in scale.
\item \code{mcse_mean}, \code{mcse_sd}, \verb{mcse_q*}: Monte Carlo standard errors of the posterior mean,
sd and quantiles (Vehtari et al. 2021). They say whether the reported digits are
supported, which is adequacy, not convergence. \code{mcse_sd} also assumes a finite fourth
moment of the draws (Pareto k below 0.25), which is not checked.
\item \code{acf1}, \code{acf10}, \code{acf50}: autocorrelation at lags 1, 10 and 50 of the kept draws,
averaged over the chains.
\item \code{geweke_z}, \code{geweke_p}, \code{hw_p}, \code{hw_discard}: tests of the START of the chains. R-hat
cannot see a short, extreme initial transient (the ranks bound its influence and it
inflates the within-chain variance); these two can. The Geweke (1992) z compares the
mean of the first 10\% with the last 50\%, with the spectral density at zero estimated
ONLY in the final window and pooled over the chains, and is combined across chains
(the sum of the per-chain z over \code{sqrt(M)} and the sum of their squares, Bonferroni over
the two). The Heidelberger and Welch (1983) Cramer-von Mises test sums the Brownian
bridges of the chains; its first look is the test, and \code{hw_discard} is the smallest
share of the kept draws (10\% to 50\%) whose removal passes. Both p-values carry the
noise of the estimated spectral density through a t reference (a chi-square scale
mixture for the Cramer-von Mises statistic) with a quarter of the effective size of the
final window as degrees of freedom, a rule tuned by simulation and not derived: on a
stationary single AR(1) chain whose first window held 4 to 21 effective draws (seven
settings), the normal reference rejected 6\% to 11\% of the time at 5\% (Geweke) and the
plain first look 7\% to 11\% (Heidelberger-Welch); with it, 3\% to 8\% and 3.5\% to 6\% (400
replicates per setting, about 1 point of Monte Carlo error;
\code{validation/mcmc_diagnostics_calibration.R}, section A). \code{window_ess} is the effective
size of the first window, summed over the chains. These numbers differ from
\code{coda::geweke.diag()}, which estimates the spectral density in each window separately
and lets the transient inflate its own variance.
\item \code{start_scale}: the scale those two tests ran on. Their reference for the noise of the
spectral density assumes draws with a finite fourth moment; on heavy-tailed stationary
draws (inverse gamma with Pareto k 0.5 to 0.83, an AR(1) copula with such a margin, t
with 1.5 df) the raw tests called the burn-in too short in 6.5\% to 15\% of the runs at the
1\% level (400 replicates per setting, section J). So the draws stay \code{"raw"} while the
Pareto k-hat of their second half (the reference window of both tests) is at most 0.25,
a quantity whose draws are all positive goes to \code{"log"} above that (the scale Gelman and
Rubin 1992 recommend for variances), and the normal scores of the ranks (\code{"rank"}) are
the last resort, noted in \code{notes}; on the same draws the verdict then said
\code{"burn-in too short"} 0\% to 2.2\% of the time. The switch is limited to heavy tails
because it costs power: a start 50 sd above a positive quantity with light tails was
caught 86\% (one chain) and 100\% (4 chains) of the time on the raw scale, and would have
been 36\% and 98\% in log and 0\% and 14\% on the ranks (50 replicates). On a heavy-tailed
quantity a start far above the posterior is a handful of huge draws: the raw tests
caught it every time, but they also flag the stationary runs above; in log it is a
short transient, caught 6\% of the time, which barely moves the median and quantiles
that the heavy-tail note points to.
\item \code{khat}: the Pareto k-hat of the two tails (Vehtari et al. 2024), as
\code{posterior::pareto_khat()}. Above 0.5 the draws may have infinite variance, the
empirical mean and its MCSE are unreliable, and both MCSE columns are left blank (0.7,
the threshold of \code{posterior}, is for Pareto-smoothed estimates, not for the raw mean).
It is \code{NA} with 29 or fewer draws in all (each tail then has 5 draws or fewer, too few
for the fit) and when both tails are constant; \code{posterior} returns \code{-Inf} in both cases.
}

Multivariate (\code{multivariate}): the multivariate ESS of Vats, Flegal and Jones (2019) by
batch means replicated over the chains (Gupta and Vats 2020) with the lugsail correction
(Vats and Flegal 2022), the minimum ESS they require (\code{min_ess}), the multivariate PSRF of
Brooks and Gelman (1998) with their factor \code{(1 + 1/M)} for \code{M} chains (\code{coda::gelman.diag()}
uses \code{(1 + 1/p)} for \code{p} parameters; the two agree only when \code{M = p}), the R-hat of every
pair of chains (the larger over the parameters) and the chains whose smallest pair R-hat
exceeds \code{isolated}. The multivariate numbers are information: the multivariate ESS can
exceed the smallest univariate one (a fast direction compensates a slow one), so it does
not enter the verdict. An isolated chain does, with the per-parameter rule below; it
mostly fires together with a disagreement, and alone (above the ESS floor) in at most 3\%
of the runs of the simulated settings where a chain was shifted or rescaled.

Locations (\code{locations}, \code{worst_levels}): the classic R-hat of every breeding value and fixed
effect from the per-chain means and variances \code{gibbs()} keeps. It does not split the
chains, so it cannot see a trend within a chain, and its Monte Carlo error has only
\code{M - 1} degrees of freedom, so with 2 chains the limit is high: for 860 levels and
\code{tau_reference} 40 it is 1.07 at 5000 kept draws per chain and 1.19 at 1800, against
1.03 and 1.09 with 4 chains (section E5), and 4 chains are the usual choice. With \code{Q}
levels over all the location terms the largest R-hat grows with \code{Q} even at convergence,
so a level is flagged only above \code{max(rhat, R_Q)}, where \code{R_Q^2} is the \code{1 - alpha/Q}
quantile of
\verb{(n-1)/n + tau chi2_\{M-1\}/((M-1) n)}, the null distribution of a level whose
autocorrelation time is at most \code{tau = tau_reference}. The levels do not share one
autocorrelation time (that of a breeding value grows with its distance from zero in
posterior sd), so \code{tau_reference} is the larger of the typical one (from the median over
the levels of the term, \verb{ess_typical = M n / tau}) and a ceiling. In the Gaussian sampler,
which draws all locations given the components and then the components given the
locations, the autocorrelation time of a location is at most that of a function of the
components plus 2 (Liu, Wong and Kong 1994), and the slowest row of the table stands in
for that function (with every component held, the locations are independent draws). The
stand-in is a heuristic: a nonlinear function of several components can mix slower than
each of them. The limit was calibrated on simulated chain means with a valid ceiling given
(the true largest time plus 2); the ceiling as a fit computes it was checked on simulated
data of 860 animals in three models, where the largest level time over that of the
slowest row was 0.17 (animal model), 0.33 (direct-maternal, chains far from mixed) and
0.20 (a two-coefficient reaction norm with 5 records per animal; sections G and G2): no
genomic model, no probit, nothing at scale. In the probit model the latent liabilities make a
third block, the ceiling does not hold, and only the typical time is used, without
calibration. A flagged level mixes slower than the ceiling allows or its chains disagree,
and one case no run length fixes is a fixed effect whose posterior is improper, such as a
covariate that separates the two categories in the probit model (a contemporary group
with every record in one category is refused by \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} before the chain). \code{ess_low} says
that the typical effective size of a term is below \code{ess_per_chain} per chain by a
one-sided test at \code{alpha} (Bonferroni over the terms) on the median over its levels: with
few levels that median is nearly one chi-square with \code{M - 1} degrees of freedom, and the
point estimate alone flagged 7\% to 25\% of the runs with independent levels in terms of 1
to 3 levels, where the test flagged none in 2000 (its power there is low too: 5\% to 41\%
at a quarter to half of the floor; section E4). \code{active} is \code{FALSE} when the limit sits
above \code{sqrt((n-1)/n + 1/M)}, the R-hat of a level with one chain one posterior sd away
from the others: the check could not flag even that level, which happens when a
component mixes slowly, and a note says so instead of letting \code{flagged = 0} read as a
pass. \code{worst_levels} holds the ten largest location R-hat of each term; in a group with
more than one effect per level (direct and maternal, \code{indirect()}, the coefficients of
\code{rn()}) each name carries the effect in parentheses, \code{"F016 (maternal)"}, as the \code{term}
column of \code{\link[=solutions]{solutions()}}.

The status of a row is the most severe condition it triggers, and every condition it
triggers is written in \code{reasons}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"not mixed"}: a chain that does not move while the others do (or a sampled column that
moves in no chain); or R-hat at or above \code{rhat} with the chains apart beyond their
autocorrelation (\code{disagreement} or \code{disagreement_tail} above its threshold, or a chain
whose R-hat with every other exceeds \code{isolated}, from 3 chains) and at least
\code{ess_per_chain} bulk draws in the weakest chain. Below that floor a disagreement cannot
be told from noise: on stationary chains with 10 to 26 bulk draws per chain, without
the floor, 12.5\% to 61\% of the runs would be \code{"not mixed"} from the disagreements alone
and 37\% to 97\% counting the isolated chain (4 and 8 chains, section C); the row is
\code{"run longer"} instead.
\item \code{"burn-in"}: the Geweke or Heidelberger-Welch test rejects at family-wise level \code{alpha}
(Bonferroni over the parameters and the two tests).
\item \code{"run longer"}: bulk- or tail-ESS below \code{ess_per_chain} per chain (\code{M} chains need
\code{M} times as many), fewer than 6 kept draws per chain (the halves are then too short
to estimate an ESS, and the verdict would rest on the R-hat alone), the split R-hat of
a single chain at or above \code{rhat} (its two halves disagree), or an R-hat at or above
\code{rhat} across chains with no chain set apart, which the ESS explains: R-hat^2 is about
1 + 2M/ESS even at convergence, and at 100 bulk draws per chain more than a third of
converged runs pass 1.01 (37\% to 42\% with 2, 4 and 8 chains, section H).
\item \code{"ok"}, or \code{"fixed"} for a column the model holds: a \code{kernel(fixed =)} component, the
probit residual, every component under \code{theta_fixed}, and a derived row when every
component is held. For raw draws, \code{"fixed"} is a column constant on the scale of its
values.
}

The verdict is the worst row, in the order \code{"chain stopped"} > \code{"not mixed"} >
\code{"burn-in too short"} > \code{"run longer"} > \code{"ok"}; a flagged location, or a location term
with \code{ess_low}, makes it at least \code{"run longer"}. On a \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} fit, a covariance group
(two or more terms joined by a \code{cov()}: direct and indirect, direct and maternal, the
coefficients of \code{rn()}) with more than 1\% of the kept draws of a chain within \code{1e-6} of a
singular correlation matrix (its smallest eigenvalue; for two terms, \verb{|cor| > 0.999999})
makes it at least \code{"not mixed"}, with that reason first (after a stopped chain, if any):
the chain sits on the boundary of the covariance matrices, where the default prior of
\code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} gives the posterior infinite mass, and a longer chain does not leave it (see
\code{prior} in \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}}). Under a posterior with no infinite mass there, and whose
correlation is not itself at +-1, the share of draws that close to the boundary is of the
order of \code{1e-6}. The groups and chains are in \code{boundary}. \code{"chain stopped"} means a chain
of \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} stopped early (an inadmissible theta or a failed Cholesky inside the chain): it is
left out of the pooled results and of every diagnostic here. With one chain (the default
of \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}}) the R-hat is the split R-hat of its two halves, and the between-chain
checks (disagreement, isolated chain, location R-hat) are inactive: run \code{chains = 4} for
them.

The thresholds are rules of thumb, not calibrated error rates, with three exceptions
measured by simulation, on stationary Gaussian AR(1) chains and, for the burn-in tests,
also on heavy-tailed draws: the burn-in tests at \code{alpha}, the location limit and the
\code{ess_low} test. What the verdict did on simulated chains
(\code{validation/mcmc_diagnostics_calibration.R}, sections B to D, J and K): with three
independent parameters on stationary Gaussian chains of 200 to 600 bulk draws per chain
(200 replicates per setting), \code{"not mixed"} never came out with 1, 2, 4 or 8 chains and
\code{"burn-in too short"} at most 2\% of the time; at 10 to 53 per chain, never \code{"not mixed"};
on the heavy-tailed draws of section J, \code{"burn-in too short"} at most 2.2\% and never
\code{"not mixed"}; with 4 to 7 draws per chain, never \code{"ok"}. With one of 4 AR(1) chains
shifted by 0.5 and by 1 posterior sd, \code{"not mixed"} 83\% and 100\% of the time, while a
shift of 0.25 sd was not told from noise (2\%); with one of 2 iid chains at 1.5 or 2 times
the sd of the other, 100\% (69\% at 1.25 times; 100 replicates). A start 50 sd away with
no burn-in gave \code{"burn-in too short"} every time with 4 chains and 86\% of the time with
one (50 replicates), where R-hat passed 1.01 in 2\% of the 4-chain runs.
}
